---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { ArrowUpRight, Thermometer, CloudRain, Snowflake, Info } from '@lucide/astro';
---
<Layout title="ENSO & the U.S. Winter Outlook" active="winter"><div class="page-shell subpage"><div class="page-intro"><div><span class="eyebrow">NORTH AMERICA · SEASONAL CONTEXT</span><h1>A winter outlook.<br /><span>Not a weather promise.</span></h1><p>Understand what ENSO can tell you, and what it cannot.</p></div></div><div class="content-notice"><Info size={18} /><div><strong>No current seasonal forecast is published on this preview.</strong><p>The regional notes below describe broad historical context. For current probabilities, open the CPC seasonal outlook.</p></div></div><section class="winter-layout"><div><span class="eyebrow">TWO DIFFERENT QUESTIONS</span><h2>History is a starting point.<br />The official outlook goes further.</h2><p class="body-copy">ENSO is one influence on a U.S. winter. The strength, timing, and location of tropical Pacific warmth can differ between events. Other ocean and atmospheric patterns also matter.</p><p class="body-copy">A seasonal temperature or precipitation outlook describes probabilities across a large region and a multi-month period. It does not tell you whether a particular weekend will be snowy.</p><a class="primary-button" href="https://www.cpc.ncep.noaa.gov/products/predictions/long_range/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Current CPC seasonal outlook <ArrowUpRight size={17} /></a></div><div class="winter-side"><Snowflake size={44} stroke-width={1} /><h3>Rain is not snow.</h3><p>Seasonal precipitation probabilities do not directly describe snowfall totals. Temperature, elevation, and individual storms affect what reaches the ground.</p></div></section><section class="regional-section"><span class="eyebrow">BROAD HISTORICAL CONTEXT · EL NIÑO WINTERS</span><h2>A regional lens</h2><div class="regional-grid"><article><Thermometer size={24} /><h3>Northern United States</h3><p>Some El Niño winters have favored warmer conditions across parts of the northern U.S. This is a tendency, not a uniform outcome.</p></article><article><CloudRain size={24} /><h3>Southern United States</h3><p>A more active southern storm track can favor wetter conditions in parts of the South. Timing and local outcomes vary.</p></article><article><Snowflake size={24} /><h3>Western mountains</h3><p>ENSO alone is not a reliable snow forecast for an individual resort. Use local forecasts for travel and mountain conditions.</p></article></div></section><div class="source-callout"><Info size={20} /><div><strong>Read the probabilities for the region and season you need.</strong><p>Historical impact guidance: NOAA Climate.gov. Current outlook: NOAA / CPC.</p></div><a href="https://www.climate.gov/enso" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NOAA ENSO resources <ArrowUpRight size={15} /></a></div></div></Layout>
